"K. Bailey" schrieb:
> 
> "tomandcatherine" <tomandcatherine@comcast.net> wrote in message news:<02R9b.481718$o%2.215211@sccrnsc02>...
> > "K. Bailey" <cheepicus@hotmail.com> wrote
> >
[how many deaths are potentially prevented by the Sokoban amulet of
reflection]
> The main other issue I would bring up with counting a record
> file is how often do wands contribute to death in indirect
> ways?

Hardly ever. There's the occasional "while helpless" which was caused
by a wand of sleep, but if reflection/no reflection makes the
difference between life and death, the ray attack gets the killing
credit almost always.

> If you get ambushed by orcs with a wand of striking
> that hurts the worst, what is the chance the death blow
> will come from the wand?

2/3 or more i'd say, notwithstanding that the specific example is
immaterial (reflection doesn't deflect striking/force bolt).

> The "oR is not an amulet of live saving that only fires
> X% of the time and then disappears, it grants you complete
> immunity to several nasty attacks. I don't think quibbling
> about the value of X is central to the point;

It is pretty important to have an idea of how large the danger is it
saves you from - poison resistance saves you from a possible outright
death (the poison was deadly...), but this sort of death triggers very
rarely and the resistance is easy to acquire. Perhaps only things that
_breathe_ poison should grant poison resistance, because this resist
is so very powerful?

An oilskin cloak prevents instant death via drowning, yet an oilskin
cloak has practically zero life-saving value, because this sort of
death is entirely avoidable by taking sufficient care, and even if you
aren't careful the risk is extremely slim.

So, to find out the "value of X":
My 3.3.x logfile lists 150 deaths in total, of which 10 where due to
actual ray attacks. I think i can assume 5 more caused by stupidity
near a floating eye, getting hit with an unresisted sleep ray and the
like. That's 15 deaths which would have been prevented by reflection;
now add to this the potential deaths between Oracle and Castle which
were prevented by the Sokoban amulet alone, say ten more. Assuming
that most of those who were saved from death via reflection got
themselves killed anyway, i get 25/150 deaths which _could_ be
prevented by reflection, that's one in six.

And the next question is what _sort_ of deaths it prevents. Ray deaths
are hardly ever due to faults on the player's side, many if not most
of them are stereotypical unavoidable ones (anything with massive kill
power that you don't resist). Something which lowers the number of
intensely annoying surprise-you're-dead experiences hardly qualifies
as "too good" for me.

[K.Bailey]
> > > > > > 2. Replace the amulet or reduce its probability
> > > > > > by having more reward level possibilities. Magic
> > > > > > marker? Wand of polymorph? Nice potions?
> >
> 
> Is the gnomish wine cellar bad because it's inconsistent with
> the other levels?

By this token, the "library" Mine_End is also inconsistent. The mine
end levels are different, there's no problem with that. And the
guaranteed prize is always the same - a luckstone and a selection of
valuable gems. None of them replaces the luckstone with something
else.

> I think insisting that the gift be some sort
> of permanent object is to no purpose. And anyway the level you
> gain with a !oGL is pretty much permanent isn't it?

Yes, but it's also not immensely valuable in the long run. There's not
much difficulty in getting the required experience to go on the quest
the old-fashioned way.

> > This certainly doesn't justify making it more likely to get a nearly
> > worthless item though, nor does it justify making it more likely to get an
> > only half-as-good item.
> 
> I was rebutting that the reward had to be EQUALLY useful
> to everyone.

You're beating up a straw man. Neither i nor "tomandcatherine" argued
for something that had to be _precisely_ as valuable for everyone, but
that the "branch rewards" (excepting the Quest, which isn't the same
for everyone) shouldn't be widely different in value for different
characters.

> > > To be clear I best like the idea of adding more rewards (one or two new
> > > ones) to shrink the "oR probability.
> >
> > You still haven't made the case for why the "oR should be less likely.
> > But, I'll look at the possibilities you suggest anyway.
> 
> I've mucho made the case.

Sorry, i just don't see it that way. I don't see how the presence of
the Amulet in Sokoban is "too much" of something. It helps preventing
a load of primarily frustrating deaths, giving me the option of
depending a small but valuable bit less on sheer dumb luck in the
mid-game (this luck mainly being the absence of insta-death-capable
wands and black dragons). That i don't get the amulet _all_ the time
already sufficiently balances it in my opinion. 

And Reflection isn't _that_ rare, either; i think about half of those
who don't get the Sokoban amulet have reflection anyway by the time
they reach Medusa's level (i.e. before taking Perseus' statue into
account), thanks to random generation of reflecting items and wishes.

> > Most people seem to view the two as
> > about equal prizes, with perhaps only a minor preference one way or the
> > other.  So I see very little reason to put any effort into changing
> > Sokoban to reduce the chances of a prize that you (and very few others)
> > find "too good".
> 
> Actually most (of the few) responses didn't say much either way
> or were tangential. Nice try at a put-away though ;)

I tend to think that it's not the status quo which needs wide explicit
support to justify its continued existence, but rather the idea of
change which needs popularity to justify the effort of effecting the
alteration.

Of course, you could simply do it the good old Patch way - make a
patch implementing your ideas and publish it.

